 "imp-f-belfast-castle-place": {
  "imdbID": "tt1741500",
  "imdbRating": "5.0",
  "imdbVotes": "103",
  "Poster": "https://m.media-amazon.com/images/M/MV5BNjAyNTYyZjYtYzc5YS00NjJkLWIxYmItYmI2MTIwZjg0YWVhXkEyXkFqcGdeQXVyMjM5NTI4Mzc@._V1_SX300.jpg",
  "Ratings": [
   {
    "Source": "Internet Movie Database",
    "Value": "5.0/10"
   }
  ],
  "Plot": "By 1897, the Lumieres and most of the other major film makers had settled on a standard look for city center street shots: people walking, carriages and two street cars coming by. The second stops, people dismount and the streetcar stands for a few more seconds and the film ends.This set format gave the movie audience something to expect and a chance to absorb details: the types of carriages, the signs and advertising -- the second streetcar advertises a bakery -- and a look at what the citizens of the particular city wear. In Belfast there are a lot more cloth caps than in, say, Paris, but one man in a tall silk hat gets off the tram."
 },
