 "imp-f-d-fense-du-drapeau": {
  "imdbID": "tt1741535",
  "imdbRating": "4.9",
  "imdbVotes": "105",
  "Poster": "https://m.media-amazon.com/images/M/MV5BY2U1NWQ1M2MtZTIyNi00OTFmLWJkYjQtOWNiMjg0OGM0MDRhXkEyXkFqcGdeQXVyMjA0NzcwMjI@._V1_SX300.jpg",
  "Ratings": [
   {
    "Source": "Internet Movie Database",
    "Value": "4.9/10"
   }
  ],
  "Plot": "La défense du drapeau (1897) This here is a very fascinating and entertaining film from the Lumiere Brothers that shows them getting away from the actuality films and entertaining scripted material. The camera is set up to the right and we get a battle scene going on. As the title suggests, the soldiers are defending the French flag. Throughout the 55-second running time we see several men shot, wounded and for the most part it's a pretty entertaining battle. Of course, over a decade later D.W. Griffith would perfect the battle sequences with films like THE BIRTH OF A NATION but what we have here is certainly well-done for the era and it has to be one of the earliest examples of a staged battle sequence. Film buffs will certainly enjoy this early movie."
 },
 "imp-f-d-fil-de-jeunes-filles-au-lyc-e": {
  "imdbID": "tt2458884",
  "imdbRating": "4.7",
  "imdbVotes": "121",
  "Poster": "https://m.media-amazon.com/images/M/MV5BMzQ5MTNiYWUtZDBjZi00YTUzLWExY2MtZGIxY2JiNDk3ZTRmXkEyXkFqcGdeQXVyMjM5NTI4Mzc@._V1_SX300.jpg",
  "Ratings": [
   {
    "Source": "Internet Movie Database",
    "Value": "4.7/10"
   }
  ],
  "Plot": "Gabriel Veyre, a cameraman who roamed over the world for Lumiere, offers us a schoolgirl exercise in deportment.This one was probably shot in Mexico -- at least the IMDb says so and the tiny amount of information available makes it seem likely. It's a very formal study in motion as the girls, each holding a stick above her head, march and countermarch by the camera in some sort of a schoolyard.The deliberate order of this movie renders it less interesting to me than the more naturalistic pieces that the Lumieres were producing. Likewise, the competing lines of movement, each part of the deliberate pattern, is less engaging."
 },
