 "imp-f-i-une-rue-tokyo": {
  "imdbID": "tt0343133",
  "imdbRating": "5.0",
  "imdbVotes": "87",
  "Ratings": [
   {
    "Source": "Internet Movie Database",
    "Value": "5.0/10"
   }
  ],
  "Plot": "This film is the work of cameraman-director (at this point the two jobs had not clearly diverged) Tsunekichi Shibata. He may be the first Japanese film maker and he released through Lumiere.This is one of two scenes of Tokyo Streets this year and it follows the pattern of most Lumiere downtown shots. However, it is a thoroughly primitive scene. There is no street car, but there is a rickshaw. People look quizzically at the camera.Which street is it? I have no way of knowing. I have seen works by Ozu shot in the mid 1930s which purport to be the Ginza -- it looked like an old exurban town center. This is even earlier. The streets are dirt.We accept as an article of faith that things change, but if we keep watching, we can't really see the change. Oh, a building goes up here, a little taller than its predecessor. That field is changed into a park, perhaps, or a small development of homes. Yet we accept each change as minor and often don't understand the creeping change that converts a mud street into a glass building that reaches eighty floors into the sky.Here it is. An unnamed street in Tokyo. Here am I, looking at this movie that was filmed the year my grandfather was born. Or maybe he was four. They didn't keep good records in Russia. I can't even see a family relationship between this street and modern Tokyo."
 },
